<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{@site.locale}}">
<head>

	{{!-- Basic meta only. Every other meta tag, OG tag and the JSON-LD block are
	emitted by {{ghost_head}} at the end of this block — hand-rolling any of them
	duplicates Ghost's output and gscan flags it. --}}
	<title>{{meta_title page=(t " (Page %)")}}</title>
	<meta charset="utf-8">
	<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

	<link rel="preload" as="style" href="{{asset "built/screen.css"}}">
	<link rel="preload" as="script" href="{{asset "built/main.js"}}">

	{{!-- The @font-face rules are compiled into screen.css from src/styles/fonts.css,
	which stays the single source of truth for the two families. The font files are NOT
	preloaded: {{asset}} always appends ?v=<content hash> (core/frontend/meta/asset-url.js),
	so a preload href can never match the bare url() a static stylesheet requests, and the
	browser would fetch every preloaded face twice. Matching them means re-declaring all 24
	faces in a template partial — see DECISIONS.md. --}}
	<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="{{asset "built/screen.css"}}">

	{{!-- The publication's accent colour drives the theme's one chromatic token. --}}
	<style>
		:root { --mirror-accent: {{@site.accent_color}}; }
	</style>

	{{!-- Resolve the appearance before first paint so a dark visitor never sees a
	light flash. Precedence: ?mode= (support/debug hook, documented in README.md) >
	the visitor's stored choice > the publication's default_appearance setting. --}}
	<script>
		(function () {
			var stored;
			try {
				stored = window.localStorage.getItem('mirror-mode');
			} catch (e) {
				/* private mode or storage disabled — the setting stands */
			}
			var preference = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('mode')
				|| stored
				|| '{{@custom.default_appearance}}'.toLowerCase();
			document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark', preference === 'system'
				? window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches
				: preference === 'dark');
		})();
	</script>

	{{!-- A template's own additions to <head>, via {{#contentFor "head"}}. Only the Layout
	override templates for the error page use it, for their robots tag. --}}
	{{{block "head"}}}
	{{ghost_head}}

</head>

{{!-- The shell holds the document; the chrome belongs to the Surface.

Each template opens with the bar its Surface wears and closes with the foot, because the
shape of both is a property of the Surface — the listing bar and the reading bar are
different heights, the members Surfaces carry a slim bar and no foot at all — and the
Surface is the template. A layout can only pick one shape, and every way of guessing which
from here is unsound: the render context of a Surface depends on the buyer's routes.yaml
(this install's Feed reports `archive`, a bare install's reports `index`) and an error page
reports no context at all. See partials/chrome/header.hbs. --}}
<body class="{{body_class}} bg-bg font-body text-text flex min-h-screen flex-col">

	{{{body}}}

	{{!-- The code block's language strip.

	assets/js/main.js clones this once per code block that declares a language and writes the
	language into the empty span. It lives here, in the template, because the two labels are strings
	a visitor reads and every one of those goes through {{t}} — a partial cannot hold them, because
	the block they belong to arrives inside {{content}} as opaque HTML.

	Both keys are lower case, unlike "Copied" on the share button, because the design sets this one
	in lower case and a text-transform is the wrong tool for a translated string.

	Nothing here renders on its own: a <template> is inert, so a visitor with scripting off gets no
	strip rather than a dead one. --}}
	<template id="mirror-code-strip">
		<div class="mirror-code-bar">
			<span class="mirror-code-lang"></span>
			<button
				type="button"
				class="mirror-code-copy"
				aria-live="polite"
				data-copy="{{t "copy"}}"
				data-copied="{{t "copied"}}">{{t "copy"}}</button>
		</div>
	</template>

	<script src="{{asset "built/main.js"}}"></script>

	{{!-- Syntax highlighting, for the code blocks that declare a language.

	Fetched only when the document holds one. Twelve of this theme's Surfaces never carry code, and
	this is the one asset that can be skipped on them outright: no code block, no request. On a post
	that has one, a reader gets 14 KB of highlighter plus only the grammars its languages need.

	The import is dynamic on purpose. {{asset}} keeps the ?v= content hash and resolves under a
	subdirectory install, and microlighter loads each grammar with a template-literal import that
	resolves against ITS OWN url — which is exactly what a bundler would break, silently, because
	the loader answers a 404 grammar with `null` and no highlighting. See
	scripts/ghost/copy-microlighter.mjs.

	`selector` scopes the scan to the content layer, which is the same set assets/js/main.js dresses
	with a strip — the platform's own UI (search, Portal, comments) is nobody's code specimen.

	The aliases are the ones a Ghost author is likely to type into the editor's language field that
	microlighter does not already know: `diff`, whose grammar is named git-diff, and Handlebars,
	which has no grammar and reads acceptably as HTML. A language with no grammar at all is not an
	error — the block keeps its strip and stays one colour, which is what the design draws anyway. --}}
	<script type="module">
		if (document.querySelector('.mirror-content pre > code[class*="language-"]')) {
			const { highlightAll } = await import('{{asset "built/microlighter/index.js"}}');
			await highlightAll({
				selector: '.mirror-content pre > code',
				languageAliases: { diff: 'git-diff', hbs: 'html', handlebars: 'html' },
			});
		}
	</script>

	{{ghost_foot}}

</body>
</html>
